const e="national-eisteddfod-south-africa",l=2026,t="Read: the free-to-download Prospectus 2024-2026 Part 1 (rules, fees, awards, video guidelines, registration, about 62 pages) and Part 14 (hard copy forms), and the public pages of eisteddfod.co.za (fees and closing dates, video and art dates, how to participate, adjudication criteria, contact, and the public overview of each of the 11 Prospectus sections). The full rule books of the sections (Parts 2 to 12) are behind a login and could not be read, so the competition rows carry only what the public section pages print, and each says which Part has the rest. Conflicting values: Part 1 prints Standard closing 30 June and Late Bird 31 July 2026; the website (newer) moved Standard to 1 August 2026 for the school holidays and prints Late Bird 15 August 2026, so the website values are used. Part 14 prints R280 for an individual hard copy video or art entry at the final deadline (19 September); the website prints R295 for video or art hard copy and 18 September, and the website value is used. Not copied: the bank name, branch, account number (only the payment method is kept), the names of donors and sponsors, previous winners, and the YouTube account screenshots steps. The Part 1 phases (Intermediate Grades 4 to 6, Senior Grades 7 to 9) differ from the template groups Entry Level (0 to 3), Junior (4 to 7) and Senior (8 and above); both are written as printed. The Gold, Silver and Bronze percentage limits are not printed; Diploma is 91 percent and higher. Fee amounts and dates are Variables. The Young Performer Awards and Young Artist Award are separate events, one line each under other.",a=JSON.parse(`{"Title":"Rules & Criteria","Subtitle":"National Eisteddfod of South Africa. A no-limits arts platform for all ages. Read these rules before you register.","NoticeTitle":"Please Read Before Registering","Notice":"Entries for 2026 open on {{EntriesOpenDate}}. Standard entries close on {{StandardDate}}. Events run {{SeasonMonths}}. Register and enter online for a lower entry fee. Do not enter both online and on paper.","Notes":["Participants are not compared with each other and no winners are announced in the adjudication sessions.","Any changes to dates or fees are announced on the website and on the Facebook page.","By entering, a participant accepts the Rules and Regulations of the National Eisteddfod Academy and accepts the decision of NEA management on all matters about the event as final."],"Sections":[{"Key":"general","Title":"About the Eisteddfod","Items":["The National Eisteddfod of South Africa is run by the National Eisteddfod Academy (NEA), a non-profit company. The name is a registered trademark.","It is an annual youth arts development platform. It is not a competition. It gives young and old a non-competitive place to perform, learn and show their talent in music, dance, theatre and fine arts.","Every performance is adjudicated by members of the NEA Adjudicators' Forum with a focus on individual achievement. Participants are not compared with each other and no winners are announced in the sessions.","The annual Eisteddfod is held in various regions, mostly from August to October, mainly at schools and community venues. Sessions are usually after school hours, from {{SessionHours}}.","A regional event is feasible only with enough entries (at least 30 entries in a genre) and a venue in the region.","Categories: speech and drama; dance; classical and crossover music (vocal and instrumental); contemporary music (vocal and instrumental); fine arts and creative work; and learners with different abilities. All categories include learners with different abilities.","Sections of the Prospectus: Confidence Builder, Indigenous Arts, Music (Novice and Elementary Instrumental, Choral, Vocal, Instrumental, Music Theatre), Dance, Speech and Drama, Creative Arts, and Participants with Different Abilities.","There is no limit to the number of entries a participant may enter.","Observe the time allowed per item in the Prospectus. A performer must not go over the maximum time. Adjudicators may stop any performance that goes over the limit.","Observe copyright where it applies.","The NEA and its host venues are not responsible for any loss, theft or injury at the festivals.","Follow the Rules and Regulations and the guidelines at the start of each section of the Prospectus.","A copy of own choice material must be available if the adjudicator asks for it.","Choice of material: vulgar language is generally not accepted in any category. Material that could disturb the audience may be allowed only if it is an integral part of the text, can be justified in context, and the NEA is told in advance so it can plan the schedule. The performer must warn the audience before the presentation.","No questionable content or language is allowed at public events such as Showcase events and all sessions of the Young Performer Awards.","No refreshments or smoking are allowed in the venue. Switch off cell phones in the venue.","Treat all officials and participants the way you would like to be treated. Venue controllers allow no changes to the program.","Entrance fees at venues pay for venue assistants and basic venue fees. See Registration Fees."]},{"Key":"eligibility","Title":"Who May Enter","Items":["The Eisteddfod is open to everyone, of all ages, including participants with different abilities. Any individual, group, school or organisation may register and enter, for an unlimited number of entries.","Participants may enter for singing, dancing, acting, reading and public speaking, playing an instrument, and painting, drawing, sculpture and similar art.","Entries may be in all official languages.","Each participant is adjudicated on his or her own performance, and not on what other learners of the same age are thought to do. This is not negotiable.","Learners are entered by school grade, not age (Dance section rule).","Phases: Foundation phase is Grades R to 3. Intermediate phase is Grades 4 to 6. Senior phase is Grades 7 to 9. FET phase is Grades 10 to 12. Primary school level is any mix of Grades 0 to 7. Secondary school level is any mix of Grades 8 to 12. Open section is out of school, past Grade 12.","The three entry categories of the template are Entry Level (Grades 0 to 3), Junior (Grades 4 to 7) and Senior (Grade 8 and above).","The highest grade of a participant decides the grade for a group.","No participant may enter and perform the same item in more than one area of the National Eisteddfod in one year.","All participants entered under the name of a school must be real learners of that school. Only schools, institutions and studios that registered with the NEA are named on certificates.","Schools and studios name each other where it applies. Studios give the name of the school for each participant. Participants are scheduled by the region chosen by the school.","A parent may not register for a school or studio. Only authorised representatives of a school or studio may register an online profile for it."]},{"Key":"registration","Title":"How to Register and Enter","Items":["Step 1: register. Go to www.eisteddfod.co.za, choose Login/Register, then Register Online, and fill in the registration form. Registration is free. It is needed to process entries, schedules and invitations.","Choose Individual registration if you are a parent or a single participant. Choose School if you are an authorised representative of a public or independent school. Choose Studio if you own or represent a studio. Community organisations and tertiary institutions also have an option.","Use the full official name of the school or institution (for example not an abbreviation or a nickname). Individuals write SURNAME, Name. Names are printed on certificates as you type them, so check the spelling.","Choose the province, region and local area. The local area decides the venues where you take part. Some events (classical and contemporary music, dance) may be grouped at a venue in a nearby area, sometimes with a higher venue fee.","When registration is saved, a user code and password are shown. Keep a printout. If you lose them, write to {{RegisterContact}}.","If the message Registration already exists appears, the name is on the database already. Write to {{RegisterContact}}. Individuals add initials or a date of birth to make the name unique.","First login: update the Billing Account and save. Returning users update their online profile every year.","Registered users can view and download the Prospectus online. Parts 1 and 14 can be downloaded without registering.","Step 2: add participants. Adding a participant (a profile) and adding an entry are two different steps. An entry is a participant linked to an item. You cannot add an entry without a participant.","Step 3: add an entry. Choose the entry type and an item number (or type the item number directly), choose the participant, choose the mode of performance (live or video), add the title of the song, poem or dance, and save. Note the payment reference code and the fee shown.","Choose the trainer or studio that coached the item, so that it is printed on the certificate.","Online entry: lower fee. Hard copy entry (paper, email or hand delivery): higher fee and an earlier closing date. Do not submit entries online and on paper, or you get duplicates and pay twice.","Hard copy entries: use the right form (registration form, Confidence Builder form, individual entries, entries with the same item number for up to 10 participants, small group for 2 to 10, large group for 11 or more, and the special request forms). Send the completed forms with proof of payment by email to {{EntryContact}}, or deliver them by hand to the NEA office.","Put the correct category and item number on the form and spell names correctly. It is the participant's job to check that the entry is right. The NEA is not responsible for wrong entries.","Individual forms: one form may carry several entries of one participant. A small group form is for entries of 2 to 10 participants with the names and contact details of all. A large group form is for 11 or more, one entry per form, with the exact number of members (send the names in Excel or Word for certificates for each member). Give the date of birth and cell phone number of each participant where you can.","Entry fee or proof of payment must come with the entry. No entry is processed if fees are outstanding.","Late hard copy entries (paper or email) after the closing date are accepted with a late entry charge and proof of payment. Online entries stay open until the online deadline at no extra cost.","Special requests about scheduling go on the Special Request Form and must reach the NEA by the closing date for entries. The NEA tries to help but cannot promise. Notes on the entry form are ignored.","Block out unavailable dates only for pre-planned activities that are fixed and known in advance (for example provincial or national events and external exams).","Special entries: a participant who missed an event as scheduled follows the special entry procedure published on the website. Changes of performance date cost extra. No other rescheduling requests are allowed.","Participation details are published online on your profile and are emailed only to bodies that asked for it on their registration profile, 2 weeks before participation. Do not wait for it to start preparing. It is the participant's job to give the correct entry number. Once the schedule is final, no changes are possible. Contact the NEA if you have no notice at least 7 days before the start of events in your region.","Changes to entry numbers or names: write to {{EntryContact}} with a copy of the participant slip that has the wrong data. A reprint of a certificate costs {{CertificateReprintFee}}.","Video entries are a recording of a performance that would normally be on stage. They suit remote areas. Unprepared items (unprepared reading, improvisation and similar) and reading items cannot be video entries.","Video: record only for this Eisteddfod (no recordings from other events). Put the camera where the adjudicator would sit, without zoom or movement, with the whole performance area visible, a plain background, good front light and clear sound. The recording must have a good balance between the voice or instrument and the accompaniment. No editing is allowed, such as correcting intonation.","Video label: hold up a page (A3 or A4, thick black marker) with your name and surname, grade and school, entry number and a short description of the item, record it for a count of 10, then pause, move to the starting position and record the whole performance without stopping. Start again from the label if anything goes wrong.","Send the video as an unlisted YouTube link (marked made for kids) by email to {{EntryContact}}, one entry and one entry form at a time, with proof of payment. The subject is the participant number, name, surname and item number (for large groups the group name, school or studio name and item number). WeTransfer and Dropbox are not accepted. Do not edit, change or delete the video after you send the link.","Or send the video on a flash disk or DVD by hand, mail or courier: one performance on each disk, each disk marked with the participant's name and surname, with a copy of the entry form and proof of payment. Check that the file plays. Rename the file as participant number, name, surname and item number.","Video and art entries are adjudicated in three sessions: {{VideoArtSessions}}","Video entries are adjudicated at the NEA office or at a venue arranged by the NEA office. Feedback is emailed to the participant."]},{"Key":"fees","Title":"Entry Fees and Charges","Items":["The fee depends on the number of participants, the date of entry, the way of submitting (online or hard copy) and the mode (live or video). To get a fee, the proof of payment must show a date before the cut-off date of that entry type.","Confidence Builder: {{ConfidenceBuilderFee}}","Individual entry: {{IndividualFee}}","Ensemble group (2 to 5): {{EnsembleFee}}","Small group (6 to 10): {{SmallGroupFee}}","Medium group (11 to 20): {{MediumGroupFee}}","Large groups: {{LargeGroupFee}}","Video or art entry: {{VideoArtFee}}","Entry fees are not refundable. The correct amount must come with the entry.","Pay by direct bank deposit or internet banking (EFT). Quote the payment reference (PayRef) code or the participant's name and the school or studio name as the reference. Email the proof of payment to {{EntryContact}}. The bank account details are printed by the NEA and are not copied here.","Entrance fee at venues: {{VenueEntranceFee}}","A reprint of a certificate after an incorrect entry: {{CertificateReprintFee}}. Printed certificates of video entries can be collected at the NEA office or posted through PostNet for {{PostNetFee}}. Write to {{AdminContact}}."]},{"Key":"dates","Title":"Important Dates","Items":["Entries for the 2026 season open on {{EntriesOpenDate}}.","Online Early Bird closes on {{EarlyBirdDate}}.","Standard entries (online and hard copy) close on {{StandardDate}}.","Online Late Bird closes on {{LateBirdDate}}. Online Lost Bird closes on {{LostBirdDate}}.","Final deadline for video and art entries is {{FinalDeadlineDate}}.","Video and art submission sessions: {{VideoArtSessions}}","Season: {{SeasonMonths}}.","Hosting events: a registered school or institution that wants to host and enter live events after the closing dates may email {{InfoContact}} to ask for an extension."]},{"Key":"venue","Title":"Where Events Take Place","Items":["Live performances are scheduled at available venues by the region and local area chosen at registration. Events that need special venues (classical music, contemporary music with backing tracks, dance) may be at a suitable venue in the region.","Venues are mainly schools and other community places that are willing to host.","A registered school may host eisteddfod events if it says so on its registration form: cannot host, willing to host for all participants, or willing to host for its own learners only.","The NEA office is at {{OfficeAddress}}. Entries, art works and videos may be sent to {{PostalDeliveryAddress}}. Office hours: {{OfficeHours}}."]},{"Key":"levels","Title":"Rounds and Next Steps","Items":["The annual Eisteddfod (August to October) is adjudication by region. Top achievers are named at regional Showcase concerts.","The NEA Young Performer Awards Competition (March to May) is a separate competition. Recipients of a diploma (90 percent and higher) in the NEA during the previous year may enter it, for the categories where they got the diploma, except unprepared, improvised or creative work. It has four rounds: First Round, Quarter Final, Semi-Final and Final.","A diploma for creative arts qualifies the artwork for the Young Artist Award Competition in the following year."]},{"Key":"judging","Title":"Adjudication","Items":["Adjudicators are members of the NEA Adjudicators' Forum. NEA management appoints them and accepts their decisions as final.","Each performance is rated against the skills, knowledge and understanding that the art form needs. The ratings are Excellent, Good, Acceptable and Below Expectation.","The ideal or typical level is when all basic requirements of the art form are met and done with ease. It lies well above the minimum level.","An adjudicator never gives false feedback and always considers the achievement of each participant within his or her own stage of development.","No one may approach the adjudicator before, during or after a session, especially to talk about the result. Then the proceedings stop, the person is told to leave and may not attend further performances.","Participants are not compared with each other and no winners are announced in the adjudication sessions.","Adjudicators write the marks (percent) for use by the NEA office only. No percentage marks are on the feedback to participants."]},{"Key":"points","Title":"Certificates and Awards","Items":["Every participant gets a certificate: Diploma, Gold, Silver or Bronze, by the criteria below.","Diploma: an excellent performance above the ideal level on most yardsticks. It need not be perfect but shows originality (spark), creativity and sincerity. Diploma level is 91 percent and higher. Inside the diploma, the adjudicator may use a scale of 1 to 10 (for the NEA office only). A special Junior Diploma is given to learners in the Foundation Phase (Grades 0 to 3).","Gold: a good performance, well above the minimum level on most yardsticks, with a high level of skills, knowledge and understanding and some initiative or creativity.","Silver: an acceptable, satisfactory performance, just above the minimum level on most yardsticks. The basic skills are present but the candidate has not full control yet.","Bronze: a performance below expectation, below the minimum level on more than one yardstick.","The syllabus prints no percentage limits for Gold, Silver and Bronze.","Point values the NEA suggests for schools: Certificate 1, Silver 5, Gold 10, Diploma 30, Item winner 10, Category winner 30, Ambassador Award 90, number 1 on the regional Top 10 90, Overall Best Individual Achievement in a genre (regional) 90."]},{"Key":"prizes","Title":"Additional Awards","Items":["Item winner certificate: the participant with the highest mark in an item in a region, with at least diploma level (91 percent and higher). All entries of an item are grouped, even across sessions.","Medal award: the participant with the highest mark in a category in a region (a category is a group of similar items, with at least 10 participants or groups), with diploma level (91 percent and higher). A medal and a Category winner certificate. A tie gets a medal for each. Each participant in a winning group item with 5 or fewer participants gets a medal. Only one medal goes to a larger group (16 or more).","NEA Ambassador Award: for the highest marks in any item, counting all regions, at most 3 percent of the total number of individual participants and groups in the year. No Ambassador Award for Grades 0 to 3 or for any reading item.","Top 5 to 10 Junior and Senior Award (regional): the best participants of each region ranked by total achievement, excluding group work. It needs diploma level in at least one event and an item or category win. Only the highest result in an item counts.","Prestige Awards: Overall Best Individual Achievement in a genre in each region, for Junior and Senior in speech and drama, dance, classical music, contemporary music, crossover music, creative work and learners with different abilities (group items excluded). A framed certificate with a seal.","Overall Best Regional Achievement for primary schools, secondary schools, pre-primary schools, schools for learners with different abilities and studios, from the total points of all their participants.","Overall Best Performing School in NEA activities (national): the school with the highest total, awarded at the final event of the NEA Awards in the following year.","Special awards are presented at the Showcase concerts. The NEA may change the minimum needs of any award without notice."]},{"Key":"complaints","Title":"Complaints and Feedback","Items":["Send complaints in writing, with the adjudicator's report form, within 7 days of the event, for attention of the CEO of the National Eisteddfod Academy, to {{InfoContact}} or by fax {{OfficeFax}}.","No complaint is dealt with by telephone.","Issues that can be solved without delay are solved at once. Do not expect written feedback in such cases."]},{"Key":"reporting","Title":"Certificates and Results","Items":["Certificates carry the name of the school and of the studio or trainer if they were given on the entry form.","Printed certificates for video entries can be collected on request or sent by PostNet.","Participation slips are emailed to registered bodies that ask for them on the registration form.","Confidence Builder entries are not recorded and no results are given."]},{"Key":"other","Title":"Other NEA Competitions and Notes","Items":["NEA Young Performer Awards Competition (separate event): for diploma recipients of the previous year, held March to May. In 2026 the online Early Bird closed on 6 February, Normal on 27 February and Late Bird on 4 March, with individual fees from R425 to R500.","NEA Young Artist Award Competition (separate event, three grade levels: Entry Level Grades 0 to 3, Junior Grades 4 to 7, Senior Grade 8 and Open): for art diploma recipients of the previous year. Artworks were due on 17 April 2026 and winners were announced on 14 June 2026.","Preliminary Qualifying Round (for the Young Performer Awards, for a limited time): a video entry with a special non-refundable fee, for people who did not qualify or did not take part in any eisteddfod in the previous year.","Participants of other festivals who got a diploma level (90 percent and higher) result may enter the Young Performer Awards on a hard copy form with proof of the result.","The Part 1 Prospectus also describes the NEA Showcase Concerts, the Arts Development Project for schools, and the Adjudicators' Forum (open to South African citizens)."]}],"Documents":[{"Title":"Prospectus 2024-2026 Part 1: Overview, Fees and Rules (Word)","Url":"https://eisteddfod.co.za/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/1-NE-Prospectus-2024-2026-Part-1-Overview-Rules-Complete.docx"},{"Title":"Prospectus 2026 Part 14: Registration, Entry Forms and Fees (Word)","Url":"https://eisteddfod.co.za/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/14-NE-Prospectus-2026-Part-14-Registration-entry-forms-and-Fees.docx"},{"Title":"Documents, entry forms and fees","Url":"https://eisteddfod.co.za/documents/"},{"Title":"Fees and closing dates","Url":"https://eisteddfod.co.za/closing-date-for-entries/"},{"Title":"Adjudication criteria","Url":"https://eisteddfod.co.za/adjudication_criteria/"},{"Title":"Young Performer Awards rules and regulations (PDF)","Url":"https://eisteddfod.co.za/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/NEA-Awards-Rules-and-Regulations-Updated-2026.pdf"}]}`),o=[{ParticipantType:"Individual",Source:"Prospectus 2024-2026 Part 1 Rules, hard copy entries",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["An individual entry is one participant. The fee is {{IndividualFee}}","Use the Entry Form for Individual Entries on paper. One form may carry several entries of one participant."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- An individual entry is one participant. The fee is {{IndividualFee}}
- Use the Entry Form for Individual Entries on paper. One form may carry several entries of one participant.`},{ParticipantType:"Team",Source:"Prospectus 2024-2026 Part 1 Rules, hard copy entries; eisteddfod.co.za fees page",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Ensemble and small group entries (2 to 10 participants) use the small group form with the names and contact details of all.","Medium and large group entries (11 or more) use the large group form, one entry per form, with the exact number of members. Send the names in Excel or Word for certificates for each member.","The highest grade of a participant decides the grade for the group.","Group fees by size: ensemble (2 to 5) {{EnsembleFee}} Small group (6 to 10) {{SmallGroupFee}} Medium group (11 to 20) {{MediumGroupFee}} Large groups {{LargeGroupFee}}"],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Ensemble and small group entries (2 to 10 participants) use the small group form with the names and contact details of all.
- Medium and large group entries (11 or more) use the large group form, one entry per form, with the exact number of members. Send the names in Excel or Word for certificates for each member.
- The highest grade of a participant decides the grade for the group.
- Group fees by size: ensemble (2 to 5) {{EnsembleFee}} Small group (6 to 10) {{SmallGroupFee}} Medium group (11 to 20) {{MediumGroupFee}} Large groups {{LargeGroupFee}}`}],n=[{AgeGroup:"Entry Level",Source:"Prospectus 2024-2026 Part 1 Definition of phases; eisteddfod.co.za adjudication criteria",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Grades 0 to 3 (the Foundation Phase is Grades R to 3). Learners in the Foundation Phase get a special Junior Diploma.","No Ambassador Award for Grades 0 to 3."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Grades 0 to 3 (the Foundation Phase is Grades R to 3). Learners in the Foundation Phase get a special Junior Diploma.
- No Ambassador Award for Grades 0 to 3.`},{AgeGroup:"Junior (Gr 4-7)",Source:"Prospectus 2024-2026 Part 1 Definition of phases",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Grades 4 to 7. The NEA phases are Intermediate (Grades 4 to 6) and Senior (Grades 7 to 9); primary school level is any mix of Grades 0 to 7."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Grades 4 to 7. The NEA phases are Intermediate (Grades 4 to 6) and Senior (Grades 7 to 9); primary school level is any mix of Grades 0 to 7.`},{AgeGroup:"Senior (Gr8+)",Source:"Prospectus 2024-2026 Part 1 Definition of phases",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Grade 8 and above. Secondary school level is any mix of Grades 8 to 12. The Open section is for people out of school, past Grade 12."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Grade 8 and above. Secondary school level is any mix of Grades 8 to 12. The Open section is for people out of school, past Grade 12.`}],r=JSON.parse(`[{"Title":"Confidence Builder","Source":"eisteddfod.co.za Confidence Builder page","Sections":{"About":["A non-competitive offer for participants who want to perform for the joy of it, without the pressure of being graded. It gives brief feedback on what you achieved.","It also helps people who enter the main eisteddfod to practise, and schools in remote areas or schools that enter many participants at the school (terms apply; contact the NEA office).","Own choice categories: speech and drama (solo verse speaking, poetry ensemble, prepared reading) and music (vocal solo with piano accompaniment only, instrumental solo with acoustic instruments only)."],"TimeAndFormat":["Strictly 2 minutes for each performance."],"Rules":["There is no limit to the number of Confidence Builder events a participant may enter.","Entries are scheduled and adjudicated at the start of the eisteddfod in all regions.","All entries must be in before the closing date. No late entries are accepted for this offer.","Entry fee: {{ConfidenceBuilderFee}}"],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":["Grading or awards (no diploma, gold or other certificate), results, and verbal feedback after the session.","Consideration for Showcase events or awards."],"Judging":["Each participant gets a brief written report and a certificate of participation."],"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- A non-competitive offer for participants who want to perform for the joy of it, without the pressure of being graded. It gives brief feedback on what you achieved.\\n- It also helps people who enter the main eisteddfod to practise, and schools in remote areas or schools that enter many participants at the school (terms apply; contact the NEA office).\\n- Own choice categories: speech and drama (solo verse speaking, poetry ensemble, prepared reading) and music (vocal solo with piano accompaniment only, instrumental solo with acoustic instruments only).\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Strictly 2 minutes for each performance.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- There is no limit to the number of Confidence Builder events a participant may enter.\\n- Entries are scheduled and adjudicated at the start of the eisteddfod in all regions.\\n- All entries must be in before the closing date. No late entries are accepted for this offer.\\n- Entry fee: {{ConfidenceBuilderFee}}\\n\\n## Not allowed\\n- Grading or awards (no diploma, gold or other certificate), results, and verbal feedback after the session.\\n- Consideration for Showcase events or awards.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Each participant gets a brief written report and a certificate of participation."},{"Title":"Indigenous Arts","Source":"eisteddfod.co.za Indigenous Arts page","Sections":{"About":["For art performed by indigenous people of South Africa (in particular the San and Nama), and by cultures that came to the south from the 16th century. It also means home-grown art.","It encourages indigenous dance, music, theatre and storytelling and the use of indigenous languages. It keeps tradition alive and builds pride in cultural heritage.","It sets adjudication standards for indigenous practices."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":null,"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":["The detailed rules are in Part 3 of the Prospectus, which is behind the login."]},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- For art performed by indigenous people of South Africa (in particular the San and Nama), and by cultures that came to the south from the 16th century. It also means home-grown art.\\n- It encourages indigenous dance, music, theatre and storytelling and the use of indigenous languages. It keeps tradition alive and builds pride in cultural heritage.\\n- It sets adjudication standards for indigenous practices.\\n\\n## Notes for participants\\n- The detailed rules are in Part 3 of the Prospectus, which is behind the login."},{"Title":"Music - Novice and Elementary Instrumental","Source":"eisteddfod.co.za Novice and Elementary page","Sections":{"About":["Instrumental music only, for junior and senior students, classical and contemporary. Novice means less than 18 months of teaching on an instrument, no matter what method.","The 18 months are the total experience in music. A new flute student who played the recorder for 2 years is not a Novice.","Elementary Level is for students past the novice stage who play elementary pieces, often from tutor books or simple arrangements."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Novice solo (primary or secondary school): a short piece may be repeated, or 2 very short pieces may be played as one item.","Novice instrumental recital: study of an instrument (piano, digital keyboard, recorder, violin and so on) for less than 18 months. A program of 3 pieces. No piece that was entered separately may be played in this item.","Novice pupil and teacher duo: for students with less than 18 months of study. The teacher plays a part. It is treated as a solo for fees. Short pieces may be repeated, or 2 very short pieces may be played as one item.","Novice ensemble: for digital keyboard students with less than 18 months of study. Unison playing is allowed. Short pieces may be repeated, or 2 very short pieces may be played as one item.","Instrumental performance, instrumental recital (a program of 3 pieces; no piece entered separately may be played) and pupil and teacher duo at Elementary Level for primary and secondary school students.","Participants bring their own instruments where it applies and give the adjudicator a copy of their music before the performance."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":["The detailed rules are in Part 4 of the Prospectus, which is behind the login."]},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Instrumental music only, for junior and senior students, classical and contemporary. Novice means less than 18 months of teaching on an instrument, no matter what method.\\n- The 18 months are the total experience in music. A new flute student who played the recorder for 2 years is not a Novice.\\n- Elementary Level is for students past the novice stage who play elementary pieces, often from tutor books or simple arrangements.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Novice solo (primary or secondary school): a short piece may be repeated, or 2 very short pieces may be played as one item.\\n- Novice instrumental recital: study of an instrument (piano, digital keyboard, recorder, violin and so on) for less than 18 months. A program of 3 pieces. No piece that was entered separately may be played in this item.\\n- Novice pupil and teacher duo: for students with less than 18 months of study. The teacher plays a part. It is treated as a solo for fees. Short pieces may be repeated, or 2 very short pieces may be played as one item.\\n- Novice ensemble: for digital keyboard students with less than 18 months of study. Unison playing is allowed. Short pieces may be repeated, or 2 very short pieces may be played as one item.\\n- Instrumental performance, instrumental recital (a program of 3 pieces; no piece entered separately may be played) and pupil and teacher duo at Elementary Level for primary and secondary school students.\\n- Participants bring their own instruments where it applies and give the adjudicator a copy of their music before the performance.\\n\\n## Notes for participants\\n- The detailed rules are in Part 4 of the Prospectus, which is behind the login."},{"Title":"Music - Choral","Source":"eisteddfod.co.za Choral Music page","Sections":{"About":["A choir is a group of singers performing together under a conductor or choir master. Choirs may sing without accompaniment, with piano or other instruments, and with backing tracks in the Classical Crossover section.","The section has classical music, classical crossover music, and contemporary choir music (for example arrangements of popular songs)."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["There are no prescribed pieces. All items are own choice.","A concert programme performance has at most 3 songs. A performance of a single work has only 1 song.","A choral work may not be presented in more than one section.","A choir has at least 20 members. Smaller groups enter the vocal section.","Junior primary school choirs usually sing in unison but are encouraged to use part-singing. Senior primary school choirs are expected to sing in two or more parts.","Senior choirs are encouraged to sing at least one song a cappella.","Submit copies of the choir programme to the adjudicator before the performance. No copies of music are needed for the traditional section.","Items with live (acoustic) accompaniment and items with backing tracks are scheduled at different suitable venues. Instruments are not always at venues where backing track items are scheduled.","The NEA is not responsible if a choir was entered in the wrong place."],"Allowed":["Backing tracks, only in the Classical Crossover section."],"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":["The detailed rules are in Part 3 of the Prospectus, which is behind the login."]},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- A choir is a group of singers performing together under a conductor or choir master. Choirs may sing without accompaniment, with piano or other instruments, and with backing tracks in the Classical Crossover section.\\n- The section has classical music, classical crossover music, and contemporary choir music (for example arrangements of popular songs).\\n\\n## Rules\\n- There are no prescribed pieces. All items are own choice.\\n- A concert programme performance has at most 3 songs. A performance of a single work has only 1 song.\\n- A choral work may not be presented in more than one section.\\n- A choir has at least 20 members. Smaller groups enter the vocal section.\\n- Junior primary school choirs usually sing in unison but are encouraged to use part-singing. Senior primary school choirs are expected to sing in two or more parts.\\n- Senior choirs are encouraged to sing at least one song a cappella.\\n- Submit copies of the choir programme to the adjudicator before the performance. No copies of music are needed for the traditional section.\\n- Items with live (acoustic) accompaniment and items with backing tracks are scheduled at different suitable venues. Instruments are not always at venues where backing track items are scheduled.\\n- The NEA is not responsible if a choir was entered in the wrong place.\\n\\n## Allowed\\n- Backing tracks, only in the Classical Crossover section.\\n\\n## Notes for participants\\n- The detailed rules are in Part 3 of the Prospectus, which is behind the login."},{"Title":"Music - Vocal","Source":"eisteddfod.co.za Vocal Music page","Sections":{"About":["Classical music is serious music in a traditional or formal style, and the 18th and 19th century European style in particular.","Classical crossover music mixes two styles. The classical or traditional style is what decides whether music belongs here. Music that sounds contemporary belongs in the contemporary section. The performance should not have strong rock or belting.","Contemporary music is music that is currently written, recorded and performed (for example blues, country, funk, hip-hop, jazz, metal, pop, rock, world music)."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":null,"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":["The detailed rules are in Part 6 of the Prospectus, which is behind the login."]},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Classical music is serious music in a traditional or formal style, and the 18th and 19th century European style in particular.\\n- Classical crossover music mixes two styles. The classical or traditional style is what decides whether music belongs here. Music that sounds contemporary belongs in the contemporary section. The performance should not have strong rock or belting.\\n- Contemporary music is music that is currently written, recorded and performed (for example blues, country, funk, hip-hop, jazz, metal, pop, rock, world music).\\n\\n## Notes for participants\\n- The detailed rules are in Part 6 of the Prospectus, which is behind the login."},{"Title":"Music - Instrumental","Source":"eisteddfod.co.za Instrumental Music page","Sections":{"About":["Classical music is serious music in a traditional or formal style, and the 18th and 19th century European style in particular.","Classical crossover music mixes two styles. The classical or traditional style decides whether it belongs here. Music that sounds contemporary belongs in the contemporary section.","Contemporary music is music that is currently written, recorded and performed."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":null,"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":["The detailed rules are in Part 7 of the Prospectus, which is behind the login."]},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Classical music is serious music in a traditional or formal style, and the 18th and 19th century European style in particular.\\n- Classical crossover music mixes two styles. The classical or traditional style decides whether it belongs here. Music that sounds contemporary belongs in the contemporary section.\\n- Contemporary music is music that is currently written, recorded and performed.\\n\\n## Notes for participants\\n- The detailed rules are in Part 7 of the Prospectus, which is behind the login."},{"Title":"Music - Music Theatre","Source":"eisteddfod.co.za Music Theatre page","Sections":{"About":["For any performance with singing, dancing and acting. Examples: a cabaret style production, vocal ensemble productions (including serenade and glee), extracts from Broadway musicals, musical theatre productions and experimental musical plays."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":null,"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":["The detailed rules are in Part 12 of the Prospectus, which is behind the login."]},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- For any performance with singing, dancing and acting. Examples: a cabaret style production, vocal ensemble productions (including serenade and glee), extracts from Broadway musicals, musical theatre productions and experimental musical plays.\\n\\n## Notes for participants\\n- The detailed rules are in Part 12 of the Prospectus, which is behind the login."},{"Title":"Dance","Source":"eisteddfod.co.za Dance page","Sections":{"About":["Dance styles include acrobatics, classical ballet, modern dance, hip-hop, belly dancing and full dance productions. There is no limit on the number of dancers in an item. It includes solos, duos, trios and large groups for all ages, starting with the pre-school section (5 years and younger)."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Learners are entered by school grade, not age.","Choose music that suits the genre and the age group. Foul or abusive language is not acceptable.","Dances that are too long lose their impact. Dances that are too short leave the adjudicator wanting more.","Costumes are not prescribed. Studio attire, with or without a logo, may not be worn on stage (no studio uniform). Studio names are not printed on adjudicator documents.","Any suitable costume that suits the dancer, the dance and the music may be worn."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":["Studio attire or a studio uniform on stage."],"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":["The detailed rules are in Part 8 of the Prospectus, which is behind the login."]},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Dance styles include acrobatics, classical ballet, modern dance, hip-hop, belly dancing and full dance productions. There is no limit on the number of dancers in an item. It includes solos, duos, trios and large groups for all ages, starting with the pre-school section (5 years and younger).\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Learners are entered by school grade, not age.\\n- Choose music that suits the genre and the age group. Foul or abusive language is not acceptable.\\n- Dances that are too long lose their impact. Dances that are too short leave the adjudicator wanting more.\\n- Costumes are not prescribed. Studio attire, with or without a logo, may not be worn on stage (no studio uniform). Studio names are not printed on adjudicator documents.\\n- Any suitable costume that suits the dancer, the dance and the music may be worn.\\n\\n## Not allowed\\n- Studio attire or a studio uniform on stage.\\n\\n## Notes for participants\\n- The detailed rules are in Part 8 of the Prospectus, which is behind the login."},{"Title":"Speech and Drama","Source":"eisteddfod.co.za Speech and Drama page","Sections":{"About":["Covers solo verse speaking up to one act plays, in all official languages. All material is own choice.","The NEA does not use the label dramatised for poetry and prose.","Poetry is narrative (limerick, narrative, ballad, epic, free verse) or lyrical (lyric, sonnet, ode, elegy, free verse)."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":null,"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":["The detailed rules are in Part 9 of the Prospectus, which is behind the login."]},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Covers solo verse speaking up to one act plays, in all official languages. All material is own choice.\\n- The NEA does not use the label dramatised for poetry and prose.\\n- Poetry is narrative (limerick, narrative, ballad, epic, free verse) or lyrical (lyric, sonnet, ode, elegy, free verse).\\n\\n## Notes for participants\\n- The detailed rules are in Part 9 of the Prospectus, which is behind the login."},{"Title":"Creative Arts","Source":"eisteddfod.co.za Creative Arts page","Sections":{"About":["For new works: music composition (classical and contemporary), literary work, visual arts (fine arts, sculpture, printmaking, design, photography), traditional African arts and crafts, and audio-visual productions.","Themes for 2024 to 2026: {{ArtThemes}}"],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["All works must be inspired by one of the themes, or an own choice story. Give details of the own choice story on the entry and declaration form attached to each artwork.","All works must be original works of the participant who enters. The participant may seek advice but must create the work as it is submitted.","No item already entered in another section may be entered here.","Copyright must be respected. Tracing existing images is not allowed. Copying existing images to develop technique in training is accepted if the source is stated in the Declaration of Own Work with each art entry.","Literary works may be written or typed. A performance is not needed.","Music entries must be in staff notation (solfa notation is also accepted). A recording is not needed but may be sent with the notation.","By entering, participants allow the NEA to display or use outstanding work at Showcase events or to promote the NEA.","Enter online or on paper before the closing date, then deliver the artwork. After processing, you get a Declaration Slip by email with a unique art number. Attach a copy of the slip to the artwork. If you have no slip, complete and attach the entry form of the Creative Arts Prospectus with its declaration.","Email the proof of payment with the payment reference code or the participant's name to {{EntryContact}}.","Deliver completed artworks to the NEA office or the venue where they are adjudicated, by the submission date. Art works may be handed in at the NEA office between 08:00 and 15:00. Pack carefully.","Art entries are adjudicated in three sessions: {{VideoArtSessions}} Hand deliveries must reach the NEA office before 16:00 and online submissions by midnight on one of the dates.","Collect artworks, reports and certificates after adjudication (a notice goes on Facebook and Twitter). The NEA office can post them to the nearest PostNet branch for {{PostNetFee}}.","Fee for video and art entries: {{VideoArtFee}}","If the adjudication is at another hosting venue, that institution gives the submission dates."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":["Pictures of artworks (they are not accepted for adjudication).","A frame on the artwork. If it is framed, the NEA is not responsible for damage to the glass or frame."],"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":["Artworks sent by mail or courier may be insured against damage in transit. The NEA takes reasonable care but is not responsible for damage.","All artworks with a diploma award qualify for the Young Artist Award Competition in the following year.","The detailed rules are in Part 10 and Part 11 of the Prospectus, which are behind the login."]},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- For new works: music composition (classical and contemporary), literary work, visual arts (fine arts, sculpture, printmaking, design, photography), traditional African arts and crafts, and audio-visual productions.\\n- Themes for 2024 to 2026: {{ArtThemes}}\\n\\n## Rules\\n- All works must be inspired by one of the themes, or an own choice story. Give details of the own choice story on the entry and declaration form attached to each artwork.\\n- All works must be original works of the participant who enters. The participant may seek advice but must create the work as it is submitted.\\n- No item already entered in another section may be entered here.\\n- Copyright must be respected. Tracing existing images is not allowed. Copying existing images to develop technique in training is accepted if the source is stated in the Declaration of Own Work with each art entry.\\n- Literary works may be written or typed. A performance is not needed.\\n- Music entries must be in staff notation (solfa notation is also accepted). A recording is not needed but may be sent with the notation.\\n- By entering, participants allow the NEA to display or use outstanding work at Showcase events or to promote the NEA.\\n- Enter online or on paper before the closing date, then deliver the artwork. After processing, you get a Declaration Slip by email with a unique art number. Attach a copy of the slip to the artwork. If you have no slip, complete and attach the entry form of the Creative Arts Prospectus with its declaration.\\n- Email the proof of payment with the payment reference code or the participant's name to {{EntryContact}}.\\n- Deliver completed artworks to the NEA office or the venue where they are adjudicated, by the submission date. Art works may be handed in at the NEA office between 08:00 and 15:00. Pack carefully.\\n- Art entries are adjudicated in three sessions: {{VideoArtSessions}} Hand deliveries must reach the NEA office before 16:00 and online submissions by midnight on one of the dates.\\n- Collect artworks, reports and certificates after adjudication (a notice goes on Facebook and Twitter). The NEA office can post them to the nearest PostNet branch for {{PostNetFee}}.\\n- Fee for video and art entries: {{VideoArtFee}}\\n- If the adjudication is at another hosting venue, that institution gives the submission dates.\\n\\n## Not allowed\\n- Pictures of artworks (they are not accepted for adjudication).\\n- A frame on the artwork. If it is framed, the NEA is not responsible for damage to the glass or frame.\\n\\n## Notes for participants\\n- Artworks sent by mail or courier may be insured against damage in transit. The NEA takes reasonable care but is not responsible for damage.\\n- All artworks with a diploma award qualify for the Young Artist Award Competition in the following year.\\n- The detailed rules are in Part 10 and Part 11 of the Prospectus, which are behind the login."},{"Title":"Participants with Different Abilities","Source":"eisteddfod.co.za Different Abilities page","Sections":{"About":["For learners with special needs: deaf and hard of hearing, blind and partly sighted, physically disabled, learning disabled and intellectually challenged participants.","Many of these participants can enter any category of the Eisteddfod. Special categories are also available for participants who are deaf or hard of hearing, and for intellectually challenged participants.","On entry forms, a disability code is asked: A blind or partially sighted, B deaf or hard of hearing, C intellectually challenged, D learning disabled, E physically challenged, F not applicable."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":null,"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":["The detailed rules are in Part 11 of the Prospectus, which is behind the login."]},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- For learners with special needs: deaf and hard of hearing, blind and partly sighted, physically disabled, learning disabled and intellectually challenged participants.\\n- Many of these participants can enter any category of the Eisteddfod. Special categories are also available for participants who are deaf or hard of hearing, and for intellectually challenged participants.\\n- On entry forms, a disability code is asked: A blind or partially sighted, B deaf or hard of hearing, C intellectually challenged, D learning disabled, E physically challenged, F not applicable.\\n\\n## Notes for participants\\n- The detailed rules are in Part 11 of the Prospectus, which is behind the login."}]`),i=[],s=[{Key:"EntriesOpenDate",Kind:"date",Label:"Entries for the 2026 season open",Value:"2026-04-01",Source:"eisteddfod.co.za closing dates page"},{Key:"EarlyBirdDate",Kind:"date",Label:"Online Early Bird closing date",Value:"2026-05-31",Source:"eisteddfod.co.za closing dates page; Prospectus 2024-2026 Part 1 Program of events"},{Key:"StandardDate",Kind:"date",Label:"Standard entry closing date, online and hard copy",Value:"2026-08-01",Source:"eisteddfod.co.za entry fees page (moved from 30 June to 1 August 2026 because of the school holidays; Prospectus 2024-2026 Part 1 still prints 30 June)"},{Key:"LateBirdDate",Kind:"date",Label:"Online Late Bird closing date",Value:"2026-08-15",Source:"eisteddfod.co.za closing dates page (Prospectus 2024-2026 Part 1 prints 31 July)"},{Key:"LostBirdDate",Kind:"date",Label:"Online Lost Bird closing date",Value:"2026-08-31",Source:"eisteddfod.co.za closing dates page; Prospectus 2024-2026 Part 1"},{Key:"FinalDeadlineDate",Kind:"date",Label:"Final deadline for video and art entries, online and hard copy",Value:"2026-09-18",Source:"eisteddfod.co.za closing dates page; Prospectus 2024-2026 Part 1"},{Key:"VideoArtSessions",Kind:"date",Label:"Video and art submission sessions",Value:"Session 1: 31 July 2026. Session 2: 31 August 2026. Session 3: 18 September 2026.",Source:"eisteddfod.co.za video and art entries page; Prospectus 2024-2026 Part 1 Program of events"},{Key:"SeasonMonths",Kind:"text",Label:"Season of the annual Eisteddfod",Value:"August to October 2026, in various regions",Source:"Prospectus 2024-2026 Part 1 Program of events; eisteddfod.co.za"},{Key:"SessionHours",Kind:"time",Label:"Usual hours of adjudication sessions",Value:"15:00 to 19:00 (or as arranged with the participating schools or community)",Source:"eisteddfod.co.za overview page"},{Key:"ConfidenceBuilderFee",Kind:"fee",Label:"Entry fee: NEA Confidence Builder (1 participant)",Value:"Online: R125 Early Bird, R135 Standard, R160 Late Bird. Hard copy: R160 standard. No Lost Bird or final deadline.",Source:"eisteddfod.co.za closing dates page; Prospectus 2026 Part 14"},{Key:"IndividualFee",Kind:"fee",Label:"Entry fee: individual entry (1 participant)",Value:"Online: R225 Early Bird, R240 Standard, R270 Late Bird, R285 Lost Bird. Hard copy standard: R260. After the Lost Bird date: video only.",Source:"eisteddfod.co.za closing dates page"},{Key:"EnsembleFee",Kind:"fee",Label:"Entry fee: ensemble group (2 to 5 participants)",Value:"Online: R310 Early Bird, R325 Standard, R365 Late Bird, R390 Lost Bird. Hard copy standard: R345. After the Lost Bird date: video only.",Source:"eisteddfod.co.za closing dates page"},{Key:"SmallGroupFee",Kind:"fee",Label:"Entry fee: small group (6 to 10 participants)",Value:"Online: R390 Early Bird, R410 Standard, R460 Late Bird, R490 Lost Bird. Hard copy standard: R430. After the Lost Bird date: video only.",Source:"eisteddfod.co.za closing dates page"},{Key:"MediumGroupFee",Kind:"fee",Label:"Entry fee: medium group (11 to 20 participants)",Value:"Online: R420 Early Bird, R440 Standard, R495 Late Bird, R525 Lost Bird. Hard copy standard: R460. After the Lost Bird date: video only.",Source:"eisteddfod.co.za closing dates page"},{Key:"LargeGroupFee",Kind:"fee",Label:"Entry fee: large group entries by number of participants",Value:"21 to 30: R450 / R475 / R530 / R570 (Early Bird / Standard / Late Bird / Lost Bird), hard copy R495. 31 to 40: R465 / R505 / R570 / R610, hard copy R525. 41 to 50: R500 / R540 / R610 / R650, hard copy R560. 51 to 60: R570 / R615 / R690 / R740, hard copy R635. 61 to 80: R650 / R705 / R795 / R845, hard copy R725. 81 to 100: R730 / R785 / R880 / R940, hard copy R800. 101 to 200: R850 / R920 / R1030 / R1105, hard copy R940. Full shows and productions: R2280 / R2455 / R2780 / R2965, hard copy R2475. After the Lost Bird date: video only.",Source:"eisteddfod.co.za closing dates page"},{Key:"VideoArtFee",Kind:"fee",Label:"Entry fee: video entry or art entry (same fee for an individual and a group)",Value:"Online: R220 Early Bird, R230 Standard, R260 Late Bird, R275 Lost Bird. Hard copy: R250 standard, R295 at the final deadline (the Part 14 form prints R280 for an individual at the final deadline).",Source:"eisteddfod.co.za video and art entries page; Prospectus 2026 Part 14"},{Key:"VenueEntranceFee",Kind:"fee",Label:"Entrance fee at venues",Value:"R71 per person. Season ticket R155. R84 per person at some specialised venues for contemporary music and dancing. Free for participants, pre-schoolers and learners in school uniform.",Source:"Prospectus 2024-2026 Part 1 Other fees and charges; eisteddfod.co.za entry fees page"},{Key:"CertificateReprintFee",Kind:"fee",Label:"Reprint of a certificate after an incorrect entry",Value:"R32 per certificate",Source:"Prospectus 2024-2026 Part 1 Other fees and charges"},{Key:"PostNetFee",Kind:"fee",Label:"Posting printed certificates of video entries, or artworks, through PostNet",Value:"R109 each time, after proof of payment",Source:"Prospectus 2024-2026 Part 1 Other fees and charges; Creative Arts page"},{Key:"InfoContact",Kind:"contact",Label:"Contact: General information and complaints",Value:{Name:"National Eisteddfod Academy NPC",Role:"General information and complaints",Phone:"011 886 6005",WhatsApp:"",Email:"info@eisteddfod.co.za"},Source:"eisteddfod.co.za contact page"},{Key:"EntryContact",Kind:"contact",Label:"Contact: Hard copy entries, video entries, changes to entries and proof of payment",Value:{Name:"National Eisteddfod Academy NPC",Role:"Hard copy entries, video entries, changes to entries and proof of payment",Phone:"011 886 6005",WhatsApp:"",Email:"entry@eisteddfod.co.za"},Source:"eisteddfod.co.za contact page"},{Key:"RegisterContact",Kind:"contact",Label:"Contact: Registration and lost login details",Value:{Name:"National Eisteddfod Academy NPC",Role:"Registration and lost login details",Phone:"",WhatsApp:"",Email:"register@eisteddfod.co.za"},Source:"eisteddfod.co.za contact page"},{Key:"AdminContact",Kind:"contact",Label:"Contact: Requests about certificates, duplicate entries and schedules",Value:{Name:"National Eisteddfod Academy NPC",Role:"Requests about certificates, duplicate entries and schedules",Phone:"",WhatsApp:"",Email:"admin@eisteddfod.co.za"},Source:"eisteddfod.co.za contact page"},{Key:"OfficeHours",Kind:"text",Label:"Office hours",Value:"Monday to Friday, 08:00 to 16:00",Source:"eisteddfod.co.za contact page"},{Key:"OfficeAddress",Kind:"address",Label:"NEA office (hand deliveries of entries, art works and videos)",Value:{Name:"National Eisteddfod Academy NPC",Address:"Bergzicht Office Park, Block 3, Suite 15, Corner Christiaan de Wet Road and Rooibok Street, Allen's Nek",City:"Roodepoort",MapUrl:"",Directions:""},Source:"eisteddfod.co.za contact page; Prospectus 2024-2026 Part 1"},{Key:"PostalDeliveryAddress",Kind:"address",Label:"Postal address for sending entries, art works and videos",Value:{Name:"PostNet Strubens Valley",Address:"Shop 22 Retail Crossing, corner Nic Diederichs Boulevard and Hendrik Potgieter Drive, Wilgeheuwel",City:"Roodepoort, 1724 (PostNet phone 011 475 0452 / 0481)",MapUrl:"",Directions:""},Source:"Prospectus 2024-2026 Part 1 Contact and banking details"},{Key:"OfficeFax",Kind:"text",Label:"Fax number",Value:"011 787 3534",Source:"eisteddfod.co.za contact page"},{Key:"FacebookPage",Kind:"link",Label:"Facebook page for announcements",Value:"https://www.facebook.com/nearsa",Source:"Prospectus 2024-2026 Part 1 How to enter"},{Key:"ArtThemes",Kind:"text",Label:"Creative Arts themes for 2024 to 2026",Value:"Our fragile planet; The universal World; My happy place; Heroes; New life; My passion; Dreamscapes; Rebuilding the Future; Ancestry; Rites of Passage; or an Own Choice story with its details on the entry and declaration form.",Source:"eisteddfod.co.za Creative Arts page"},{Key:"Announcements",Kind:"news",Label:"Notices printed for 2026",Value:[{Date:"2026-04-01",Title:"2026 entries open",Text:"Entries for the 2026 season of the National Eisteddfod of South Africa open on 1 April 2026.",Url:""},{Date:"",Title:"Standard deadline moved to 1 August",Text:"Because of the school holidays from 26 June to mid-July, the deadline for Standard entries moved to 1 August 2026. The hard copy deadline also moved to 1 August 2026 for busy parents.",Url:""},{Date:"",Title:"Virtual entries discontinued",Text:"Virtual entries (a live performance on the Zoom platform) have been discontinued. Video entries are the recording of a live performance.",Url:""},{Date:"",Title:"Prospectus 2024-2026",Text:"With a few changes, the National Eisteddfod Prospectus 2024 to 2025 is valid for 2026.",Url:""},{Date:"",Title:"Changes announced online",Text:"Any extension of closing dates is announced on the website and the National Eisteddfod Facebook pages.",Url:""}],Source:"eisteddfod.co.za pages"}],d=[{Claim:"Prospectus 2024-2026 Part 1 (Overview, Fees and Rules): conditions, entry rules, adjudication, phases, awards, video guidelines, registration steps.",SourceType:"Primary - the organiser's own rules document",Source:"National Eisteddfod of South Africa Prospectus 2024-2026 Part 1 (National Eisteddfod Academy NPC)",Url:"https://eisteddfod.co.za/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/1-NE-Prospectus-2024-2026-Part-1-Overview-Rules-Complete.docx"},{Claim:"Prospectus 2026 Part 14 (Registration, Entry Forms and Hard Copy Entry Fees): the hard copy forms.",SourceType:"Primary - the organiser's own forms document",Source:"National Eisteddfod Prospectus 2026 Part 14",Url:"https://eisteddfod.co.za/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/14-NE-Prospectus-2026-Part-14-Registration-entry-forms-and-Fees.docx"},{Claim:"2026 entry fees, closing dates and the extension of the Standard deadline.",SourceType:"Primary - the organiser's own website",Source:"National Eisteddfod Academy fees and closing dates pages",Url:"https://eisteddfod.co.za/closing-date-for-entries/"},{Claim:"Adjudication criteria and awards (Diploma, Gold, Silver, Bronze).",SourceType:"Primary - the organiser's own website",Source:"National Eisteddfod Academy adjudication criteria",Url:"https://eisteddfod.co.za/adjudication_criteria/"},{Claim:"Public overview of each Prospectus section (Confidence Builder, Indigenous Arts, Novice and Elementary, Choral, Vocal, Instrumental, Music Theatre, Dance, Speech and Drama, Creative Arts, Different Abilities).",SourceType:"Primary - the organiser's own website",Source:"National Eisteddfod Academy prospectus sections",Url:"https://eisteddfod.co.za/nea-prospectus/"}],c={TemplateKey:e,Year:2026,Note:t,Page:a,ParticipantTypes:o,AgeGroups:n,Competitions:r,CompetitionAgeGroups:i,Variables:s,Evidence:d};export{n as AgeGroups,i as CompetitionAgeGroups,r as Competitions,d as Evidence,t as Note,a as Page,o as ParticipantTypes,e as TemplateKey,s as Variables,l as Year,c as default};
